      { kind: "faq", items: [{ q: "What gynaecological problems can be treated laparoscopically?", a: "Selected ovarian cysts, fibroids, endometriosis, ectopic pregnancy, pelvic adhesions and some cases requiring hysterectomy can be treated laparoscopically." }, { q: "Is laparoscopic surgery always better than open surgery?", a: "No. Laparoscopy has advantages in many suitable patients, but open surgery may be safer in very large masses, extensive adhesions, advanced disease or other complex situations." }, { q: "Can fibroids be removed without removing the uterus?", a: "Yes. Myomectomy removes fibroids while preserving the uterus, but whether laparoscopic myomectomy is suitable depends on fibroid size, number and location." }, { q: "Can endometriosis be treated with laparoscopy?", a: "Yes. Laparoscopy can be used to diagnose and surgically treat selected endometriosis. The extent of surgery should consider pain, fertility goals and ovarian reserve." }, { q: "How long is recovery after laparoscopic hysterectomy?", a: "Recovery varies, but many patients need several weeks before resuming full routine. Exact advice depends on the operation, overall health and job demands." }] }
