      { kind: "faq", items: [{ q: "Is hysterectomy the only treatment for fibroids?", a: "No. Depending on symptoms, fibroid size, age and fertility goals, options may include observation, medicines, myomectomy or other treatments. Hysterectomy is usually considered when definitive treatment is appropriate." }, { q: "Will my ovaries always be removed during hysterectomy?", a: "No. Ovaries may be preserved when appropriate. Removal depends on age, ovarian disease, cancer risk and the reason for surgery." }, { q: "Will I enter menopause after hysterectomy?", a: "If the ovaries remain, hormone production may continue until natural menopause. If both ovaries are removed, menopause usually occurs immediately." }, { q: "Can hysterectomy be done laparoscopically?", a: "Yes, many hysterectomies can be performed laparoscopically, but the safest approach depends on uterine size, previous surgery, adhesions, cancer concerns and overall complexity." }, { q: "How long is recovery after hysterectomy?", a: "Recovery varies by surgical approach. Many minimally invasive patients recover over 2–4 weeks, while open surgery may take 4–6 weeks or longer." }, { q: "Can I become pregnant after hysterectomy?", a: "No. Removal of the uterus means a pregnancy cannot be carried afterward." }] }
